    <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.75rem;margin-top:.875rem;font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">
      <span style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.3rem"><span style="width:18px;height:2px;background:var(--amber);display:inline-block"></span>AR (baseline)</span>
      <span style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.3rem"><span style="width:18px;height:2px;background:var(--cyan);display:inline-block"></span>MDLM (14×)</span>
      <span style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.3rem"><span style="width:18px;height:2px;background:var(--purple);display:inline-block"></span>RePlaid s.c. (20×)</span>
      <span style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.3rem"><span style="width:18px;height:2px;background:rgba(100,100,130,.8);display:inline-block"></span>Duo (22×)</span>
      <span style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.3rem"><span style="width:24px;height:2px;background:rgba(139,92,246,.45);border-top:2px dashed rgba(139,92,246,.5);display:inline-block"></span>RePlaid no s.c. (27×)</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  One unexpected finding: in the <em>over-trained regime</em> (training a smaller model for much longer
  than compute-optimal), RePlaid (s.c.) overtakes MDLM. For a 66M parameter model, RePlaid achieves
  lower loss than MDLM when both are pushed past their compute-optimal points. This is significant for
  production scenarios where small, cheap-to-serve models are over-trained on large data budgets.
</p>

